<AssignMessage name="AM-ScanVerdict">
  <DisplayName>Capture Model Armor scan verdicts for logging</DisplayName>
  <!--
    THIS IS THE VERSION TO RESTORE when Model Armor is reinstated. It replaces
    the "disabled"-writing stub currently in apiproxy/policies/AM-ScanVerdict.xml.

    WHY THIS EXISTS. Model Armor does not fail when content is too big for it.
    Its limits are 4 MB of text and, per filter, 65,536 tokens (prompt
    injection/jailbreak, Responsible AI, CSAM) or 130,000 tokens (SDP)
    - docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/quotas. Content over a limit comes back
    as EXECUTION_SKIPPED: a VERDICT, not an error. The API returns 200, the
    policy sees a successful call with no match, and the oversized prompt or
    response passes through COMPLETELY UNSCANNED. So the failure mode is silent
    loss of safety coverage, and nothing else would signal it.

    VARIABLE NAMES CONFIRMED from an Apigee debug session 2026-09-15 rather than
    inferred. The per-filter names this policy first used
    (raiFilterResult.executionState, piAndJailbreakFilterResult.executionState,
    sdpFilterResult.executionState) DO exist but are null in this Apigee
    version, so every logged field read "unset". The variables that are actually
    populated are invocationResult and filterMatchState, so those are what get
    logged now.

    invocationResult  SUCCESS when Model Armor was reached and returned a verdict
    filterMatchState  NO_MATCH_FOUND / MATCH_FOUND

    LIMIT, stated because it matters: these two do NOT distinguish a scan that
    was SKIPPED for size from one that ran clean; both surface as SUCCESS plus
    NO_MATCH_FOUND. The per-filter executionState needed to tell them apart is
    inside the raw responseFromModelArmor JSON, which would need its own
    ExtractVariables step to reach. Worth doing before anyone relies on this log
    to assert content was actually scanned. Until then, treat these fields as
    "Model Armor answered", not "the content was inspected".

    The ma_*_failed fields matter only if the policies are restored with
    continueOnError="true" (fail open). With continueOnError="false" a failure
    kills the call and is loud on its own.
  -->
  <AssignVariable>
    <Name>ma_prompt_result</Name>
    <Value>unset</Value>
    <Ref>SanitizeUserPrompt.SUP-SanitizeUserPrompt.invocationResult</Ref>
  </AssignVariable>
  <AssignVariable>
    <Name>ma_prompt_match</Name>
    <Value>unset</Value>
    <Ref>SanitizeUserPrompt.SUP-SanitizeUserPrompt.filterMatchState</Ref>
  </AssignVariable>
  <AssignVariable>
    <Name>ma_response_result</Name>
    <Value>unset</Value>
    <Ref>SanitizeModelResponse.SMR-SanitizeModelResponse.invocationResult</Ref>
  </AssignVariable>
  <AssignVariable>
    <Name>ma_response_match</Name>
    <Value>unset</Value>
    <Ref>SanitizeModelResponse.SMR-SanitizeModelResponse.filterMatchState</Ref>
  </AssignVariable>
  <AssignVariable>
    <Name>ma_prompt_failed</Name>
    <Value>unset</Value>
    <Ref>SUP-SanitizeUserPrompt.failed</Ref>
  </AssignVariable>
  <AssignVariable>
    <Name>ma_response_failed</Name>
    <Value>unset</Value>
    <Ref>SMR-SanitizeModelResponse.failed</Ref>
  </AssignVariable>
  <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</AssignMessage>
